    h1: "How Does Springfield Plan Review Differ from Greene County?",
    answer: "Direct answer: building inside Springfield city limits means permitting through the City of Springfield, where plan review runs through the city's Building Regulations staff and the development checklist includes a sinkhole report and sinkhole permit under the city's sinkhole ordinance, floodplain permits where applicable, and a land disturbance permit for grading work. Building in unincorporated Greene County means permitting through the county's Resource Management office, with its own application forms, review staff, and inspection scheduling. The two paths ask for similar technical information but route it to different reviewers on different timelines, and a parcel's jurisdiction is decided by its legal location and annexation status, not by its mailing address. The AHJ decides what is approved, so the first engineering task on any Springfield-area project is confirming which government has jurisdiction over the site.\n\nThe practical differences show up in the submittal package. Springfield's development review coordinates building, zoning, public works, and stormwater comments through the city's process, and its design standards checklist explicitly calls out items like the sinkhole report, floodplain development permits, and sewer extension details, including county highway department sign-off where sewers run in county right-of-way outside city limits. Trade permits for electrical, plumbing, mechanical, and gas work layer on top of the building permit in both jurisdictions, and those are typically pulled by licensed contractors rather than the owner. Unincorporated county review tends to involve fewer overlapping city departments, but it still requires engineered site plans, grading review where county rules trigger it, and coordination with state agencies for items like highway access or stream crossings.\n\nAnnexation is the trap to watch. Parcels on the edge of Springfield may carry a Springfield mailing address while sitting in unincorporated Greene County, and voluntary or involuntary annexation can move a project from county to city jurisdiction mid-stream, changing the reviewers, the fee schedule, and the applicable amendments. Sewer projects are a common trigger: extending city sewer to serve a county parcel often requires an annexation petition and consent as part of the deal, which the city's design standards anticipate in their sewer extension checklist. The engineer verifies jurisdiction from the parcel's legal description and the current city boundary map at project kickoff, not from what the listing agent said.\n\nFor the design team, the workflow difference is mostly about sequencing. Under city review, the geotechnical report, sinkhole report, stormwater calculations, and structural plans need to be coordinated early because the city's checklist reviews them as a package, and a missing sinkhole report can hold up an otherwise complete submittal. Under county review, the engineer confirms which county permits apply to the specific scope, since a simple addition and a new commercial site go through different levels of review. In both cases, plan-check corrections are a normal part of the process, and responding with complete, clouded revisions keeps the review moving. Neither jurisdiction promises an approval on any timeline; the reviewers decide, and the engineer's job is to give them a submittal that answers questions before they are asked.",
