    description: "Fayetteville enforces water-quality and detention standards over karst limestone, so stormwater design detains runoff without injecting it into sinkholes.",
    h1: "How Is Stormwater Designed for Fayetteville's Karst Terrain?",
    answer: "Direct answer: Fayetteville stormwater design must satisfy the city's drainage standards while respecting the karst limestone underneath the city, which means detaining and treating runoff without injecting it into sinkholes, caves, or solution conduits. The city's requirements, set by the Unified Development Code's drainage chapter and the adopted Drainage Criteria Manual, apply to developments that add significant impervious area and cover four standards: water quality, channel protection, overbank flood protection, and extreme flood protection. The water-quality standard targets high removal of suspended solids from post-development runoff, typically through bioretention, wet ponds, or proprietary treatment, and the channel-protection standard requires the increased runoff from frequent storms to be detained and released slowly so downstream channels are not eroded. Over karst, the engineer adds a second design layer: every basin, swale, and infiltration feature is sited and detailed so concentrated water never reaches a karst feature, because a stormwater pond that drains into a sinkhole is both a design failure and a groundwater contamination pathway.\n\nThe karst constraint changes which stormwater tools are appropriate. Infiltration-based practices that work well on deep, uniform soils are risky over soluble limestone with shallow rock and known voids, since infiltrated water follows dissolution pathways directly to the aquifer with almost no filtration. Fayetteville designers therefore favor detention over infiltration on karst sites: dry and wet detention basins, extended-detention outlets, and bioretention with underdrains and liners where the geotechnical investigation indicates karst risk. The geotechnical program for a Fayetteville commercial or multifamily site maps rock depth, voids, and sinkhole history across the drainage areas, not just under the buildings, because a basin sited over a void can lose its stored volume into the ground or open a collapse. Dye tracing and geophysical surveys are used on the highest-risk sites to confirm that proposed facilities are not sitting on active flow paths.\n\nThe regulatory path runs through the city for parcels inside Fayetteville and through Washington County for unincorporated sites, and the two should not be confused. City review applies the Drainage Criteria Manual's design storms, outlet controls, and water-quality calculations, with the City Engineer as the technical authority on drainage decisions, and streamside protection rules add buffers and design constraints along regulated streams. County review follows the county's own standards and reviewers. Because Fayetteville has continued to tighten its stormwater rules over time, including attention to single-lot development that older codes exempted, the engineer designs to the requirements in effect at submittal, not to the rules from the last project. The AHJ decides what is approved, so pre-application coordination with the city's development staff is cheap insurance on a karst site.\n\nConstruction-phase water management deserves equal attention. Fayetteville's Ozark rainfall can overwhelm a half-built sediment basin in a single storm, and sediment-laden runoff that reaches a sinkhole or stream undoes the water-quality design before the project even opens. Erosion and sediment control plans phase clearing with stabilization, protect existing karst features with buffers during construction, and keep concrete washout, fuels, and chemicals away from drainage pathways. Post-construction, detention facilities carry permanent maintenance obligations, typically recorded on the plat and assigned to the owner or property owners association, because a basin that silts up or a bioretention cell that clogs stops meeting its design standards within a few seasons.",
    directAnswer: "Fayetteville stormwater design meets the city's four drainage standards, water quality, channel protection, overbank, and extreme flood protection, while keeping concentrated runoff out of the karst limestone below. That means detention over infiltration on karst sites, geotechnical mapping of voids beneath every basin, lined or underdrained treatment features, and permanent maintenance obligations recorded on the plat.",
