        heading: "The Four Standards and Karst-Safe Compliance",
        body: "Fayetteville's drainage framework asks every regulated development to control both the quality and the timing of its runoff. The water-quality standard requires capturing and treating runoff to remove a high percentage of total suspended solids, which designers meet with bioretention cells, wet ponds, permeable pavements, or manufactured treatment devices sized to the manual's criteria. The channel-protection standard addresses the frequent storms that shape streams: the added runoff volume from the one-year storm must be detained and released over an extended period, commonly around forty hours, so downstream channels see pre-development-like flow durations instead of erosive pulses. Overbank and extreme flood standards then size the system for the larger design storms, keeping structures and finished floors safe and preventing the development from worsening flooding downstream.\n\nOn karst, each standard gets a siting filter. The geotechnical investigation identifies sinkholes, voids, springs, and shallow rock across the site, and the civil engineer routes basins, swales, and outfalls away from those features. Bioretention and other filtration practices get underdrains and impermeable liners where karst is present so treated water leaves through a pipe rather than percolating into a conduit. Wet ponds are checked for leakage potential, since a pond that will not hold water is a maintenance failure and a possible karst trigger. Outlet structures are designed for controlled release rates with trash and sediment forebays, and the calculations document pre- versus post-development hydrology for the reviewer's check. Nothing in this approach conflicts with the manual; it is the manual applied with knowledge of what lies beneath.",
