        body: "The building sewer design is a small civil project with outsized consequences if it is wrong. The engineer establishes the invert elevation at the house, the invert at the public main tap, and the pipe run between them, verifying that the slope stays within the workable range: too flat and solids settle, too steep and liquids outrun solids, and either way the owner inherits chronic blockages. Where the house sits below the main, a grinder pump station in a sealed basin macerates and pumps the wastewater uphill through a small-diameter force main, with duplex pumps, alarms, and emergency storage for reliability. The plans show the full profile, cleanout locations, and the tap detail the sewer provider requires.\n\nOzark site conditions shape the construction method. Shallow dolomite and limestone mean rock trenching with hoe-rams or blasting along the pipe route, proper rock-free bedding around the pipe, and careful backfill and compaction so the trench does not become a settlement trough across the yard. Steep slopes need anchored pipe and erosion-protected trench restoration. The route avoids karst features, wells, and the old drain field's saturated zones, and it maintains the separations from water lines and structures that the plumbing code requires. The plumbing permit covers the building sewer with inspections at the trench, the pipe, and the connection, and the sewer provider inspects the tap itself. A pressure or leakage test of the new line before backfill is cheap insurance against excavating the yard twice.",
